---
import DocShell from '../../layouts/doc-shell.astro';
import { docsLinks } from '../../lib/nav';

const toc = [
   { id: 'target', label: 'The simulated screen reader in CI' },
   { id: 'browser', label: 'Install a browser' },
   { id: 'preflight', label: 'Check the runner with doctor' },
   { id: 'workflow', label: 'GitHub Actions example' },
   { id: 'baseline', label: 'Baselines for existing sites' },
   { id: 'sr', label: 'Screen reader checks' },
];
---

<DocShell
   title="Run in CI"
   summary="A CI runner has no VoiceOver or NVDA, so every a1 command that starts a screen reader needs --sr virtual. axe, tree, and audit need Chrome, Edge, Brave, or Chromium. --fail-on sets which impact level fails the build, and --baseline lets CI ignore existing violations while still failing on new ones."
   links={docsLinks}
   toc={toc}
>
   <section class="panel" id="target">
      <h2>The simulated screen reader in CI</h2>
      <p>
         <code>voiceover</code> only exists on macOS and <code>nvda</code> only on Windows, and
         neither runs unattended on a CI runner. Pass <code>--sr virtual</code>
         on every <code>sr start</code> or <code>sr walk</code> call in CI. Without
         <code>--sr</code>, <code>sr start</code> and <code>sr walk</code> start the platform's
         own screen reader, which on a macOS runner means VoiceOver.
      </p>
      <pre><code>a1 sr walk http://localhost:3000/checkout --sr virtual --json</code></pre>
   </section>

   <section class="panel" id="browser">
      <h2>Install a browser</h2>
      <p>
         <code>axe</code>, <code>tree</code>, and <code>audit</code> load the page in a headless
         browser. A CI image with no browser installed needs one added before the job runs them.
      </p>
      <pre><code>npx playwright install chromium</code></pre>
      <p>
         The simulated screen reader also needs this browser when a session opens a URL, because
         it runs inside a headless Chromium page, which is what makes page scripts, live
         regions, and dialogs work. With no Chromium-family browser, the simulated screen reader
         falls back to jsdom and page scripts do not run, which changes what a walk or an axe
         run can see.
      </p>
      <p>
         <code>a1 wcag</code> reads data bundled with the package. It starts no browser and
         makes no network request, so it needs no setup step in CI.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="panel" id="preflight">
      <h2>Check the runner with doctor</h2>
      <p>
         <code>a1 doctor --strict</code> exits 3 while a required step is missing, and prints
         the exact command for each one under Action items. Run it as an early step so a missing
         browser fails that step with the command to fix it, instead of a timeout in a later step.
      </p>
      <pre><code>a1 doctor --strict</code></pre>
   </section>

   <section class="panel" id="workflow">
      <h2>GitHub Actions example</h2>
      <pre><code>jobs:
  accessibility:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v4
      - uses: actions/setup-node@v4
        with:
          node-version: 24

      - run: npm ci
      - run: npx playwright install chromium

      - name: Start the app
        run: npm run start &

      - run: npx a1 doctor --strict
      - run: npx a1 axe http://localhost:3000/checkout --level AA --fail-on serious
      - run: npx a1 sr walk http://localhost:3000/checkout --sr virtual --out walk.md
      - uses: actions/upload-artifact@v4
        if: always()
        with:
          name: a11y-walk
          path: walk.md</code></pre>
      <p>
         <code>axe</code> exits 4 on a violation at or above <code>--fail-on</code>'s
         <a href="/guides/violations#impact">impact level</a>, which fails the step and the
         job. <code>sr walk --out</code> writes the transcript either way, and
         <code>if: always()</code> uploads it, so a failed job includes every announcement in
         order as well as the axe result.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="panel" id="baseline">
      <h2>Baselines for existing sites</h2>
      <p>
         Adding <code>a1 axe</code> to CI on a site with existing violations means the first
         run fails on all of them. <a href="/guides/scripting#baseline">Commit a baseline</a>
         once, so CI only fails on a new violation, and fix the accepted ones over time.
      </p>
      <pre><code>a1 axe http://localhost:3000/checkout --level AA --baseline a11y-baseline.json</code></pre>
   </section>

   <section class="field-note" id="sr">
      <h2>Screen reader checks</h2>
      <p>
         <code>sr goto</code>, <code>sr find</code>, and <code>sr expect</code> all exit 4 when
         nothing matches, so a step that checks one announcement fails the job the same way a
         failed axe run does.
      </p>
      <pre><code>a1 sr start http://localhost:3000/checkout --sr virtual --json
a1 sr goto --role button --name "Pay now"
a1 sr expect "button, Pay now"
a1 sr stop --json</code></pre>
      <p>
         For a test suite instead of shell steps, <a href="/guides/testing">write screen reader
         tests</a> with <code>a11ied/test</code> or <code>a11ied/vitest</code> and run them
         through the same <code>npm test</code> step as the rest of the suite.
      </p>
   </section>
</DocShell>
